  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Human System</h1>
    
    <p>
      Human System is a developing body of thought about understanding and 
      intervening responsibly in human systems when the right action depends 
      on the conditions actually present.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      The work emerges from a recurring entrypoint: consulting and technical 
      or organizational work that requires entering existing human systems, 
      understanding them, and acting in ways that improve them without 
      pretending to possess universal solutions.
    </p>
    
    <h2>Core stance</h2>
    
    <p>
      "It depends" is not the end of the answer. It is the beginning of the 
      investigation into what the answer depends upon.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Experience provides patterns and better questions. It does not remove 
      the need to understand the current context. Prior resemblance to a past 
      situation offers hypotheses, not automatic prescriptions.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Several threads run through this work:
    </p>
    
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Observing changes the system.</strong> Discovery is already 
        intervention. The act of looking alters what is being looked at.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Abstractions are useful but dangerous.</strong> Models and 
        frameworks help organize thinking, but trusting them over the reality 
        they stand for leads to interventions that fail in contact with actual 
        human systems.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Relationships are living infrastructure.</strong> They carry 
        far more than information and trust. They are the medium through which 
        change succeeds or fails.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Interventions should carry expectations.</strong> State what 
        you expect to happen. Let reality contradict it. Update the model.
      </li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>What is here</h2>
    
    <p>
      This site publishes selected papers that test propositions through 
      field experience, research, and progressively developed observations. 
      Shorter papers are the near-term vehicle for testing the thinking. 
      A book manuscript exists as one possible future synthesis, not the 
      primary output.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      The work deliberately avoids turning observations into a branded 
      framework, method, or maturity model before they have been tested. 
      Conclusions may change as evidence accumulates.
    </p>
    
    <h2>How to read this</h2>
    
    <p>
      These are not universal prescriptions. They are propositions tested 
      against experience and evidence, stated clearly but held lightly. 
      Some will resonate immediately. Others may not fit your context. 
      The intent is not to convince but to make the thinking available 
      for others working in similar tensions.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
